faqs:
  - q: "How is gratuity calculated in India?"
    a: "Gratuity is monthly wages last drawn, multiplied by 15, divided by 26, multiplied by your years of service. A part year of more than six months counts as a full year, and exactly six months does not. The amount is capped at ₹20 lakh unless your employer agrees to pay more."
  - q: "Can I get gratuity before five years?"
    a: "Permanent employees need five years of continuous service and working journalists need three. The minimum does not apply on death or disablement. Fixed-term employees qualify after one year of service under the contract."
  - q: "What is the 50% wage rule for gratuity?"
    a: "Wages under the Code are basic pay, dearness allowance and retaining allowance. If your other pay is more than half of your total pay, the part above half is added back to wages. In practice, wages become the higher of basic plus DA and half of total pay."
  - q: "Does the new rule apply to service before 21 November 2025?"
    a: "If you leave on or after 21 November 2025, the Ministry of Labour says gratuity is paid on the wages last drawn, under the Code. The Code also counts service given before it began. If you left before that date, the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972 applies."
  - q: "What is the maximum gratuity?"
    a: "The ceiling is ₹20 lakh. Your employer can pay more under an award, agreement or contract, because the Code protects better terms."
  - q: "Is gratuity taxable?"
    a: "For private sector employees covered by the gratuity law, the tax-free part is the lowest of the amount received, 15/26 of last salary (basic plus DA) for each year counted, and ₹20 lakh. Anything above that is taxed as salary. Gratuity under government service rules is fully exempt."
  - q: "Does a small company have to pay gratuity?"
    a: "The chapter covers factories, mines, oilfields, plantations, ports and railway companies, and shops or establishments with 10 or more employees on any day of the previous 12 months. Once covered, an employer stays covered even if staff numbers fall below 10."
  - q: "Can government employees use this calculator?"
    a: "No. Government employees under their own gratuity rules are outside this part of the Code, so this page does not work out their amount. Central civil employees should check the CCS rules, where the ceiling has been ₹25 lakh since 1 January 2024."
