        <div class="mt-12 space-y-8">
            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">How gratuity is calculated</h2>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">Gratuity pays 15 days of wages for every year you worked. Monthly wages are divided by 26, because the law treats a month as 26 working days, so the formula is:</p>
                <p class="qt-well font-semibold mb-4">Gratuity = monthly wages last drawn × 15 ÷ 26 × years of service</p>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">Years of service means completed years, plus one more if the part left over is more than six months. Someone with 7 years and 7 months is paid for 8 years. Someone with 8 years and exactly 6 months is paid for 8.</p>
                <p class="leading-relaxed">The total stops at ₹20 lakh. That is the legal ceiling, and an employer can still pay more under an award, agreement or contract.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">What changed on 21 November 2025</h2>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">The gratuity chapter of the Code on Social Security, 2020 came into force on this date and replaced the Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972. The formula stayed the same. What changed is who qualifies sooner and what counts as wages.</p>
                <div class="qt-table-wrap">
                    <table class="qt-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Left before 21 Nov 2025</th><th>Leaving on or after 21 Nov 2025</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">Law</td><td>Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972</td><td>Code on Social Security, 2020</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">Wages</td><td>Basic pay + DA</td><td>Basic + DA, raised to half of total pay if other pay is more than half</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">Fixed-term staff</td><td>5 years</td><td>1 year under the contract</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">Permanent staff</td><td>5 years</td><td>5 years</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">Formula</td><td>15 ÷ 26 per year</td><td>15 ÷ 26 per year</td></tr>
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">Ceiling</td><td>₹20 lakh</td><td>₹20 lakh</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">The 50% wage rule, with an example</h2>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">Many pay structures keep basic low and put most of the salary into allowances. That used to shrink gratuity. Under the Code, if your other pay is more than half of your total pay, the part above half is added back to your wages.</p>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">Take basic plus DA of ₹20,000 and other pay of ₹40,000. Total pay is ₹60,000, so half is ₹30,000. Other pay is ₹10,000 above that line, so wages for gratuity become ₹30,000 and not ₹20,000.</p>
                <p class="leading-relaxed">The Ministry of Labour says performance incentives, ESOPs and reimbursements are not part of wages. If your other pay is half of the total or less, nothing is added and wages stay at basic plus DA.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Worked examples</h2>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">All of these assume the last working day is 31 October 2026 and the exit is covered by the Code.</p>
                <div class="qt-table-wrap">
                    <table class="qt-table">
                        <thead><tr><th>Employee</th><th>Basic + DA</th><th>Other pay</th><th>Service</th><th>Years counted</th><th>Wages used</th><th>Gratuity</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody>
                            {% for e in gratuityExamples %}
                            <tr><td class="font-semibold">{{ e.who }}</td><td>{{ e.basicDA }}</td><td>{{ e.otherPay }}</td><td>{{ e.served }}</td><td>{{ e.counted }}</td><td>{{ e.wage }}</td><td class="font-bold">{{ e.gratuity }}{% if e.capped %} (ceiling){% endif %}</td></tr>
                            {% endfor %}
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Who gets gratuity</h2>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
                    <li><strong>Permanent employees:</strong> after 5 years of continuous service, when they resign, retire or the job ends.</li>
                    <li><strong>Working journalists:</strong> after 3 years.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fixed-term employees hired directly:</strong> after 1 year under the contract. A further part of more than six months counts as one more year.</li>
                    <li><strong>Death or disablement:</strong> no minimum service. On death the money goes to the nominee, or to the heirs if there is no nominee.</li>
                    <li><strong>Contract labour through a contractor:</strong> the Ministry of Labour treats this as different from fixed-term employment. The contractor pays, after five years.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mt-4">Sick leave, accidents, approved leave, lay-offs, strikes and lock-outs do not break continuous service.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Tax on gratuity</h2>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">For private sector employees covered by the gratuity law, the tax-free part is the lowest of three figures: the amount you received, 15/26 of your last salary for each year counted, and ₹20 lakh. Salary here means basic plus DA, so money added by the 50% rule can be taxable even though your employer must pay it.</p>
                <p class="leading-relaxed mb-4">The tax law has not caught up fully. The Income-tax Act, 2025 still names the repealed 1972 Act in this entry, and we found no official note on how it maps to the Code. This page assumes the same limit carries over. Check with a tax professional if a large amount is involved.</p>
                <p class="leading-relaxed">If your employer is not covered by the gratuity law and pays gratuity anyway, a different limit applies: half a month's average salary for each completed year, within a ₹20 lakh lifetime limit. This calculator does not work out that case.</p>
            </div>

            <div class="qt-card">
                <h2 class="qt-section-title mb-4">Where the answer can differ</h2>
                <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2">
                    <li><strong>Close to five years:</strong> some High Court rulings under the old Act treated 4 years and 240 days as five years. The Code does not say this, so this calculator asks for five full years.</li>
                    <li><strong>Service before the Code:</strong> some articles split your career at 21 November 2025. The Ministry of Labour's FAQ of 16 March 2026 instead says gratuity is paid on the wages last drawn under the Code.</li>
                    <li><strong>Fixed-term part years:</strong> the Central Rules round up a further part of more than six months. We found no official example for shorter parts, so they are left out here.</li>
                    <li><strong>State rules:</strong> this page follows the Code and the Central Rules. Your State's rules may word some details differently.</li>
                    <li><strong>Your contract:</strong> an employer can offer better terms than the law, and many gratuity trusts have their own rules.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>

